<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Peel Commission</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1936">1936</a>–<a href="/years/1937">1937</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Palestine Royal Commission</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Peel Commission was a British royal commission led by Lord Peel, appointed in 1936 to investigate the causes of the Arab revolt in Mandatory <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>. Its report of July 1937 concluded that the mandate was unworkable and proposed, for the first time, partition into a Jewish state and an Arab state, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> keeping <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> and a corridor to the sea. Arab leaders rejected the plan.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peel-commission.jpg" alt="Peel Commission" width="320" height="767" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: UK Government · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PeelMap.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the Arabs rejected the <b>Peel Commission</b>'s partition the revolt resumed in 1937; the British lost Nablus and Hebron for a time, then crushed it with overwhelming force, 6,000 armed Jewish auxiliary police and Orde Wingate's Special Night Squads raiding Arab villages, while the Irgun attacked Arab marketplaces…</blockquote><a href="/empire/mandatory-palestine">Mandatory Palestine · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Anger at land purchases and British rule grew into the Arab Revolt of 1936–1939; the <b>Peel Commission</b> proposed partition in 1937, and the White Paper of 1939 limited Jewish immigration.</blockquote><a href="/movement/zionism">Zionism · Course of Events</a></li></ul></section>
